       xxzz parses space-separated lists of options from the  environment  vari‐
       ables XXZZ__DDEEFFAAUULLTTSS and XXZZ__OOPPTT, in this order, before parsing the options
       from the command line.  Note that only options are parsed from the  en‐
       vironment  variables; all non-options are silently ignored.  Parsing is
       done with ggeettoopptt__lloonngg(3) which is used also for the command line  argu‐
       ments.

       WWaarrnniinngg::  By  setting  these  environment variables, one is effectively
       modifying programs and scripts that run xxzz.  Most of  the  time  it  is
       safe to set memory usage limits, number of threads, and compression op‐
       tions via the environment variables.  However, some options  can  break
       scripts.   An  obvious  example  is ----hheellpp which makes xxzz show the help
       text instead of compressing or decompressing a file.  More subtle exam‐
       ples  are ----qquuiieett and ----vveerrbboossee.  In many cases it works well to enable
       the progress indicator using ----vveerrbboossee, but in some situations the  ex‐
       tra messages create problems.  The verbosity level also affects the be‐
       havior of ----lliisstt.

