              User-specific or system-wide default options.  Typically this is
              set in a shell initialization script to enable xxzz's memory usage
              limiter by default or set the default number  of  threads.   Ex‐
              cluding  shell initialization scripts and similar special cases,
              scripts should never set or unset XXZZ__DDEEFFAAUULLTTSS.

       XXZZ__OOPPTT This is for passing options to xxzz when it is not possible to set
              the  options  directly on the xxzz command line.  This is the case
              when xxzz is run by a script or tool, for example, GNU ttaarr(1):

                     XZ_OPT=-2v tar caf foo.tar.xz foo

              Scripts may use XXZZ__OOPPTT, for example, to set script-specific  de‐
              fault  compression  options.   It  is still recommended to allow
              users to override XXZZ__OOPPTT if that is reasonable.  For example, in
              sshh(1) scripts one may use something like this:

